#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = [
#     "capabilities-contract>=0.12,<1",
#     "slack_sdk>=3.33",
#     "websocket-client>=1.8",
# ]
# ///
"""
slacker — read a Slack workspace as the linked user, over their own session.

Agent-invoked, read-only. Links the person's OWN Slack account by its web-client
session (the workspace `xoxc-` token plus the shared `d` cookie), the way the
`whatsapp` and `telegram` capabilities emulate their vendor's own client, and
reads only what that account can already see: direct messages, group direct
messages, and the public and private channels it belongs to, their message
history and thread replies. It keeps that history in the project's Postgres
store, syncs it forward incrementally, and a bundled service captures it live.

This capability never writes to Slack. It sends no messages, sets no presence,
joins no channels, and marks nothing read; it has no write verb at all. The
session is the person's own, used transparently as one more of their browser
sessions — there is no concealment, no monitoring bypass, and no client-identity
spoofing. Run `slacker doctor` first to prove the session and the workspace.

Startup protocol
  1. `slacker doctor` — proves the session authorises (`auth.test`), resolves
     the linked user and the workspace, and matches the connection's binding.
  2. Exit 2 means the session is invalid or expired (`invalid_auth`): the person
     links the connection again (its `d` cookie is short-lived; there is no
     silent refresh).
  3. Exit 6 is a configuration fault (no connection, no store); exit 4 is policy
     (a disabled capability); exit 5 is the store or Slack being unreachable.

Connections
  A connection is one workspace session of one linked account. It carries the
  env vars that hold the session secrets (token_env, cookie_env), never the
  secret values, plus the non-secret workspace identity. A machine with several
  workspaces declares one connection each. Secrets resolve through the 4-tier
  cascade (flags > project .env(.local) > ~/.config/slacker/credentials.env >
  process env); selection is `--connection <id>` (-c), else the registry's
  default, else the sole entry.

  Two ways to link. `slacker link` signs you in through a Chrome window it
  opens and reads the session out for you (needs an installed Chrome and a
  display). Or paste: read the xoxc- token and the `d` cookie from a browser
  you are already signed in to and run `connection add <id> --stdin` (see
  `slacker guide linking`). Either way the two secrets land only in the
  credentials file, never in the registry, a log, or output.

Store
  Conversations, messages, thread replies, reactions, users and the sync cursor
  live in the project's Postgres database, in slacker_* tables keyed by the
  workspace account (the team id), created and migrated on first use. The
  capture belongs to the account, not to one project: an account has one history
  in a database, whichever project on the machine reads it, so the tables carry
  no project id. The database is resolved as every store capability resolves it
  (AGENTKIT_DB_* in the project .env.local/.env, then the environment, then the
  machine's `capabilities store set`). With none, every verb that reads or writes
  the capture exits 6; help, contract and connections still answer.

Commands
  slacker help                       Print this usage block
  slacker link [<id>] [--workspace W] [--default] [--replace] [--timeout S]
               [--global|--project] [--events]
                                     Link a workspace by signing in through a
                                     Chrome window it opens: it waits while you
                                     sign in (typing and clicking nothing),
                                     reads the xoxc- token and the `d` cookie
                                     over Chrome's DevTools protocol, writes
                                     them as a connection, closes the window
                                     and deletes the temporary profile, then
                                     proves the link. Several workspaces: pass
                                     `--workspace` (team id, domain or name) or
                                     an id that matches one. No Chrome or no
                                     display: link by paste instead.
  slacker doctor [--connection ID]   Prove the session, the user and the
                                     workspace; match the binding.
  slacker whoami [--connection ID]   The linked identity and workspace, named.
  slacker conversations [--types ...] [--limit N] [--search TERM]
                                     List the conversations the account can
                                     see (im, mpim, public_channel,
                                     private_channel), newest activity first.
  slacker history <channel> [--limit N] [--oldest TS] [--latest TS] [--fresh]
                                     Read a conversation. Answers from the
                                     store, and fetches from Slack when it
                                     cannot cover the request.
  slacker thread <channel> <thread_ts> [--fresh]
                                     Read a thread's replies. Answers from the
                                     store once it holds every reply the
                                     parent counts, else fetches from Slack.
  slacker search <query> [--limit N] [--sort score|timestamp]
                                     Search message text across every
                                     conversation the account can see, as
                                     Slack's own search answers it (its query
                                     modifiers such as in: and from: apply),
                                     the store not needed. Each match names
                                     its channel, ts and thread_ts, which
                                     `history` and `thread` read. No match is
                                     count 0 at exit 0.
  slacker sync [--channel ID] [--full] [--limit N]
                                     Bring the store forward: fetch every
                                     message of each member conversation newer
                                     than the stored cursor, deduplicating on
                                     the message timestamp. --limit bounds how
                                     far back a first sync or --full reaches.
  slacker service ...                The bundled live-capture listener (see
                                     `slacker service help`).

Contract commands (shared by every capability)
  slacker connections                Resolve every connection, secrets masked
  slacker stub                       One-line summary
  slacker manifest [--json]          The capability's declaration, with the
                                     bundled capture service
  slacker guide [topic]              The guide menu, or one guide
  slacker ids list|get|set|rm        Durable, non-secret project identifiers
  slacker refs                       What this project reads for connections
  slacker inventory [--network]      What this capability holds here, local
                                     and secret-free
  slacker migrate [status]           Apply or report the slacker_* tables

Success is JSON on stdout; structured errors are JSON on stderr. Exit 4 is a
policy refusal.
"""

